\section{Three Different Things to Reuse}

Levine distinguishes Unix relinkable output produced with \verb|-r| from the IBM linkage editor's ability to replace individual control sections in an existing linked program. In the Quong--Linton study he describes, a daemon retained the symbol table and left slack between contributions so that minor edits would often fit in the original locations. A sufficiently large displacement of later contributions triggered a clean relink. The timing results belong to the systems studied, not to current toolchains \cite[chapter~11]{levine2000linkers}.

There are at least three distinct units of reuse. A partial linker reuses an intermediate object with updated symbols and relocations. A native incremental linker reuses some placement and fixup work on an already linked image. A compiler doing incremental LTO can reuse generated machine code for an IR partition and still run a fresh native link. A cache of generated objects does not guarantee stable addresses. Conversely, unchanged addresses do not guarantee that an optimized body remains valid.

\section{Invalidate Results When Their Inputs Change}

In a native incrementally linked image, suppose a relative data field at virtual address \verb|0x501040| addresses a label at \verb|0x501080| with addend zero. The value $S+A-P$ is \verb|0x40|. If an edit moves the label to \verb|0x50108c|, even within its original contribution's reserved space, the field must change to \verb|0x4c|. The referrer's object bytes did not change. Track incoming fixups and recompute from the original addend. Changes in the chosen definition can trigger the same repair without moving the old definition at all. A patching implementation must also account for alignment, section and segment extents, and metadata that describes addresses. If it cannot update them consistently, use a clean link rather than publishing a partially repaired output.

For LTO the dependency can be less visible: an imported or inlined callee can stop appearing as a native relocation. Levine's historical Tera example notes that a change to one input can require regenerating code associated with unchanged source files because of cross-file inlining. Modern compiler IR makes those dependencies explicit before code generation. In conventional full LLVM LTO, participating IR can be merged for global analysis and optimization. The \href{https://clang.llvm.org/docs/ThinLTO.html}{Clang ThinLTO documentation} describes the alternative: combine module summaries in a global index, decide which definitions may be imported, then optimize and generate code in parallel module backends. Native objects can participate in resolution but cannot be imported as though they still carried compiler IR.

