\section{Compare Warm and Cold ThinLTO Results After an Edit}

Use three handwritten C files with Clang and ELF LLD. Hidden visibility makes this helper a candidate for direct binding in the component; it is not a promise that any particular optimization will fire.

\begin{verbatim}
/* main.c: status 0 means the arithmetic returned 12. */
int reported(void);
int main(void) { return reported() != 12; }

/* report.c: this source and its bitcode stay unchanged. */
__attribute__((visibility("hidden"))) int increment(void);
int reported(void) { return increment() + 5; }

/* inc.c: change 7 to 8 in the second build. */
__attribute__((visibility("hidden")))
int increment(void) { return 7; }
\end{verbatim}

Build with these commands; start with an empty directory for the cache.

\begin{verbatim}
clang -O2 -flto=thin -c main.c report.c inc.c
clang -O2 -flto=thin -fuse-ld=lld main.o report.o inc.o \
  -Wl,--thinlto-cache-dir=warm -o before
\end{verbatim}

Check that \texttt{before} exits with status zero. Save the digest of \texttt{report.o}. Edit \texttt{inc.c} to return eight and rebuild only \texttt{inc.o}; repeat the link with the same cache and a new output name. Then repeat that link using a fresh cache, keeping the same object order and flags. Both new programs must exit with status one, and the digest of \texttt{report.o} must be unchanged. Inspect the linked functions with \verb|objdump -d| if you want to see whether the call or its result was optimized in this build. A specific cache-hit count, the absence of a symbol, or a prescribed inlining decision is not a correctness requirement. An unoptimized build gives a separate baseline for the expected exit statuses.

\Cref{fig:visual-290} shows why hashing just the caller's bitcode is not a sufficient cache key.

