\section{Cache the Resolution Assumptions, Too}

The invariant is that a backend result can be reused only if every fact it depended on still holds: its own IR, imported bodies or summaries used for decisions, target and code generation options, and the applicable symbol resolution and export constraints. A conservative key can also carry the compiler identity to avoid reusing an artifact across incompatible toolchains. A weak definition that becomes prevailing after an input-order change can invalidate imports even if neither body was edited. A symbol now needed by native code or by the dynamic interface cannot be internalized merely because no IR caller remains. A default-visible function in a shared library may be preemptible even when the participating IR shows just one body.

Test these as negative cases. Compile a new file without LTO to obtain an ordinary native object. It should define \texttt{native\_report} to return \texttt{increment()}, with the same hidden declaration as in \texttt{report.c}. Declare and call both entry points from \texttt{main.c}; after the edit, require \texttt{reported} to give 13 and \texttt{native\_report} to give 8. This native input needs a callable definition of \texttt{increment} even if the original IR callers have all inlined it.

For a separate export test, rebuild just \texttt{report.c} and \texttt{inc.c} with \verb|-fPIC| in addition to the ThinLTO compile flags. Link them as a shared library with \verb|-shared| and the same LTO driver flags, then add \verb|-Wl,--version-script=exports.map| with the script \verb|{ local: *; };|. \verb|readelf --dyn-syms -W| should show \texttt{reported} before adding the script and not after; the hidden helper should not be exported in either case. Compare results between warm and clean builds of each configuration. Byte-for-byte comparisons are an additional check when builds are configured to be reproducible, not a substitute for exercising the dependency that changed.
